nsider_buying":null,"_score_insider_buying":null,"pf":[],"description_full":"Insteel Industries Inc., together with its subsidiaries, manufactures and markets steel wire reinforcing products for concrete construction applications in the United States and internationally. The company offers prestressed concrete strand (PC strand) and welded wire reinforcement (WWR) products. Its PC strand, a seven-wire strand that is used to impart compression forces into precast concrete elements and structures providing reinforcement for bridges, parking decks, buildings, and other concrete structures. The company's WWR engineered reinforcing product is used in non-residential and residential construction. It also produces a range of WWR products, such as engineered structural mesh, an engineered made-to-order product that is used as the primary reinforcement for concrete elements or structures serving as a reinforcing solution for hot-rolled rebar; concrete pipe reinforcement, an engineered made-to-order product, which is used as the primary reinforcement in concrete pipe, box culverts, and precast manholes for drainage and sewage systems, water treatment facilities, and other related applications; and standard welded wire reinforcement, a secondary reinforcing product for crack control applications in residential and light non-residential construction, including driveways, sidewalks, and various slab-on-grade applications. The company serves manufacturers of concrete products, distributors, rebar fabricators, and contractors through a network of sales representatives. Insteel Industries Inc. was founded in 1953 and is headquartered in Mount Airy, North Carolina.","ceo_bio":"Mr. Howard Osler Woltz III | President, CEO & Chairman | Age 69 | Compensation: $2.3M (FY2025)","culture_narrative":"Employs approximately 983 people. Revenue per employee of $752K ranks in the top quartile among Industrials peers, with revenue growing 5%/yr across its filed history. Each employee generates $25K of free cash flow annually. Stock-based compensation of $4K per employee reflects an ownership culture. CEO compensation is 3\u00d7 revenue per employee (modest relative to workforce productivity). Governance score flags moderate compensation governance risk (5/10).","financial_summary":["Insteel Industries, Inc. is a small-cap industrials business with a $540M market cap on $740M of annual revenue.","The top line has compounded at +4% over the past three years and +6% annually across the full decade, which puts it in mature, slow-growth territory.","Returns on capital run at 11% against a 9% cost of capital, which means the business barely earns its keep economically, and 12% gross margins are typical of commodity economics where competitors can replicate the product cheaply.","The business throws off $25M in free cash flow \u2014 roughly 3 cents on every revenue dollar, which is the owner-earnings figure that actually belongs to shareholders, and cash conversion sits at 1.24\u00d7 so those profits are backed by real cash.","On the balance sheet, the balance sheet carries net cash, which is the gold standard: the business never has to make desperate decisions in a bad year.","Shareholder yield of 4.6% reflects a genuine commitment to returning capital rather than empire-building, and insiders hold 5.1% of the shares, and profit capture is only 0.5\u00d7 the fair share, suggesting no structural edge inside its industry."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Fragmented industry with below-average profit capture \u2014 no moat to protect margins","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Price sits 23% above estimated fair value \u2014 no margin of safety left","sev":"amber","cat":"valuation","magnitude":0.22812844392218184},{"text":"Volatile returns on capital suggest the moat is narrower than a snapshot ROIC implies","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Gross margin trails most Industrials peers \u2014 competitors appear to have the pricing hand","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Industrials sector lagging the market significantly, underperforming by 6% over 3 months","sev":"amber","cat":"sector"},{"text":"Cyclicality and capital intensity \u2014 order books turn on a dime, and the heavy fixed cost base that creates the moat also magnifies the downturn.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"Supply chain reshoring costs and labour shortages \u2014 onshoring mandates raise capex requirements while a shrinking skilled-labour pool inflates operating costs.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"Tariff and trade-policy exposure \u2014 industrial exporters sit squarely in the crosshairs of protectionist policy, and retaliatory tariffs can close markets without warning.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"}],"sector_tailwinds":["Valued at a 31% discount to sector median EV/EBITDA","Altman Z comfortably in the safe zone \u2014 a fortress balance sheet on this measure","21 consecutive years of dividend payments \u2014 an operating record that makes the dividend claim credible","Healthy payout ratio leaves room for both dividend growth and reinvestment","Among the least levered in Industrials \u2014 the fortress balance sheet in its peer set"],"news_headlines":[{"title":"(IIIN) Movement Within Algorithmic Entry Frameworks","source":"news.stocktradersdaily.com","link":"https://news.stocktradersdaily.com/news_release/1/IIIN_Movement_Within_Algorithmic_Entry_Frameworks_111126105802_1794455882.html","date":"2026-11-12","timestamp":1794455882.0,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":0.0,"tier":0,"scope":"company","tags":[]},{"title":"Insteel declares $0.03/share & special cash dividend of $1.00/share (NYSE:IIIN)","source":"seekingalpha.com","link":"https://seekingalpha.com/news/4670175-insteel-declares-003share-special-cash-dividend-of-100share","date":"2026-11-10","timestamp":1794345710.0,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":0.66,"tier":0,"scope":"company","tags":["dividend"]},{"title":"Price-Driven Insight from (IIIN) for Rule-Based Strategy","source